%S A400860 1,5,7,32,11,13,17,19,25,23,224,29,31,128,37,41,43,115,47,119,53,81,0,
%T A400860 59,61,256,67,71,73,79,187,83,203,89,209,235,97,101,103,0,107,109,121,
%U A400860 113,295,287,127,512,131,299,137,139,319,323,149,151,157,1408,163,415,167,1376,173,413,391,179,181,1504,191,193,197,199,0,515,0,211,535,2592,575,223,0,227,229,517,233,0,239,241,581,251,551,257,583,263,623,269,271,289,277,281,283,2432
%N A400860 Smallest number k such that the multiplicative group of squares modulo k is cyclic of order A396684(n), or 0 if no such k exists.
%C A400860 Write Q(m) = {k : the multiplicative group of squares modulo k is cyclic of order m}, then Q(1) = {k : (Z/kZ)* = (C_2)^r} = {divisors of 24}; for m > 1, k is in Q(m) if and only if (Z/kZ)* is of the form (C_2)^r X C_{2m}.
%C A400860 Factor m = p_1*...*p_s into prime powers. Then numbers in Q(m) are of the form d*q_{S_1}*...*q_{S_t}, where S_1, ..., S_t is a partition of {1, 2, ..., s}, q_{S_i} is a prime power such that (Z/q_{S_i})* = C_{2*Product_{j in S_i} p_j} or C_2 X C_{2*Product_{j in S_i} p_j}, and d is a divisor of 24 coprime to q_{S_1}*...*q_{S_t}.
%C A400860 More terms are shown to distinguish this sequence from A002396 even away from the zero terms. In general the smallest member in Q(m) is not necessarily the smallest k such that A002322(k) = 2*m. For example, for m = 144, we have Q(144) = {2432, 3456, 7296}, but the least k such that A002322(k) = 288 is k = 1843, with (Z/1843Z)* = C_6 X C_288. See Links for more such examples.
%H A400860 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400860/b400860.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400860 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400852/a400852.txt">Examples showing the procedure to find Q(m)</a>.
%H A400860 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400852/a400852_1.txt">List of Q(m) for the first 10000 values of m in A396684</a>.
%H A400860 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400852/a400852_2.txt">PARI program for A400852</a>.
%H A400860 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400860/a400860.txt">Values of 2m <= 400000 such that the smallest number in Q(m) is different from the smallest number k such that A002322(k) = 2m</a>.
%H A400860 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplicative_group_of_integers_modulo_n">Multiplicative group of integers modulo n</a>.
%o A400860 (PARI) my(v); for(n=1, 200, if(is(2*n), v=multgp(n); print1(if(v, vecmin(v), 0), ", "))) \\ See _Charles R Greathouse IV_'s second program in A002174 and the PARI program link for multgp
%Y A400860 Cf. A400852 (cardinality of Q(m)), A400853 (largest member in Q(m)).
